SM(533,{"n":"1 Corinthians 5","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 5.mp3","d":2067.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.12,"So we'll go ahead and uh get started."],[24.4,"And when we get started here in just a moment, we'll be picking up in uh St."],[29.28,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians chapter five, which you can see from the heading, it's gonna get interesting."],[38.0,"But before we do that, we'll go ahead and get caught up where we've been."],[42.56,"A lot of what we've read so far is the introduction to the letter and Saint Paul talking about sort of his his own role."],[51.68,"He's been arguing against the factions that have been developing at the church in Corinth, who have sort of set themselves up as followers of. individual apostles, that this is causing sort of dissension and division"],[65.08,"So he's talked about his own ministry and the humility required by that ministry."],[70.84,"He has nothing to boast about, so you certainly shouldn't become his fan or his follower."],[77.64,"And that that applies to the apostles in general, and that rather than becoming a fan or a follower of Saint Paul, you should try to emulate the humility with which he's lived his life in service of Christ, and that if you do that, following Christ by serving him in the way that St."],[97.32,"Paul had served them, served him in front of them, that then they would be very much on the right track."],[104.52,"And now we're at sort of a transition point because St."],[108.72,"Paul is about to get into particular issues."],[112.96,"And so right at the end of chapter four, he had talked about the fact that he was sending St."],[119.92,"Timothy there as sort of a representative to sort of help him out, remind them of some of the things that St."],[126.64,"Paul had taught them and the way he lived among them."],[129.12,"And this is preparatory to him coming to visit, and that's sort of a threat and a promise."],[135.44,"At the end of chapter four, he says, I'd like my visit to be warm and wonderful, but if some things don't change, it'll be unpleasant, and none of us wants that."],[146.72,"Because some things depend on the case."],[147.76,"Question R You said that usually the letters begin with general theology and then get into particular applications."],[157.02,"This seems to have been in particular applications all along, but is this the point at which it no?"],[163.9,"This is still the theology portion."],[166.22,"Okay, so it's gonna get even more specific later on."],[168.94,"Okay, because it already seemed to be specific."],[171.74,"Yes. This this letter is a little different in that as we're about to see very much, uh, there are serious problems at Corinth."],[181.9,"Uh-huh. So very serious problems in Corinth."],[186.3,"So even the theological portion is a bit more focused."],[189.98,"Uh-huh. Is a bit more focused."],[192.56,"Uh, when we read Romans, St"],[194.44,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, there was sort of one main theme dealing with the reconciliation of the Jewish Christian believers and the Gentile Christian believers in one body."],[205.8,"And so all of the theological portion and pretty much all of the practical application portion were sort of all aimed at that."],[216.28,"There's sort of this one overarching theme."],[218.44,"Corinth has a lot of problems."],[221.56,"And so while there's still generally those two big halves, both of them, rather than being focused on sort of one theme, are going to talk about a whole bunch of different things."],[234.36,"Uh-huh. That uh that all apply to their situation."],[238.05,"apply to So he's got to lay groundwork on a whole bunch of different issues and then it a whole bunch of different issues."],[247.55,"Instead of one central thing."],[249.39,"So that's the difference here."],[250.75,"And we'll see in some of St."],[252.03,"Paul's other epistles, like especially some of them are written to one person."],[256.03,"Uh huh. So they're still broadly going to have those two halves, but it's going to come across very differently because you know you're you're talking to one person and you do that differently than you talk to a community."],[268.61,"So, unless there are any other questions, comments, issues?"],[273.49,"No, I want to get to the end the of debates."],[275.89,"Okay. We'll go ahead and uh pick up here at the beginning of St."],[282.77,"Paul's epistle, first epistle to the Corinthians, chapter 5, verse 1"],[287.13,"It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you, that's such sexual immorality as is not even named among the Gentiles, that a man has his father's wife."],[299.69,"So this is bad."],[304.82,"St. Paul cuts to the chase."],[306.74,"There's sexual immorality going on there."],[309.54,"And remember, Gentiles also means nations."],[312.98,"But his point being, this isn't even, this isn't one of these things where we've talked about how some of the issues in Corinth are the fact that a lot of the converts have come from paganism."],[324.5,"Uh-huh. And so there's this massive difference between worship and piety and and what we'd call religion in the pagan, Roman pagan world and in the Christian world, which is really an extension of the Jewish world."],[340.22,"And so they had to make a lot of adaptations."],[343.36,"There were things that were perfectly appropriate before that are now wildly inappropriate."],[347.52,"This is not one of those things that was appropriate before, is the point that St."],[350.96,"Paul's making. Nobody thinks it's okay to sleep with your stepmother."],[355.92,"Uh-huh. Nobody thinks that."],[358.8,"There's nobody out there who would defend that."],[361.04,"This is not just sexual immorality in the eyes of the church, but this would have been looked at ascance by everything."],[366.64,"This would have been shameful for complete pagans."],[371.28,"And so it's not just an issue, as we're gonna see."],[375.89,"So remember, St."],[376.93,"Paul says the sexual immorality is taking place among you."],[380.69,"Yeah, I th I'm just gonna say how many people are doing this."],[384.13,"It's it's in your midst."],[385.89,"So it's one particular person who's doing this act."],[389.49,"But the problem isn't just that there's this person sinning."],[392.53,"Uh-huh. The problem is there's this person sinning in the community, and the community's not doing anything about it."],[399.73,"So it's not that this is one example among many of them."],[405.41,"Well, there's gonna be other things, but yeah."],[409.71,"But he's talking about this this but he's talking about this particular case here."],[413.51,"That this is happening, and you're not doing anything about it."],[415.31,"not doing anything about it. That is important"],[416.55,"Uh-huh. Because we talked about way back in the Acts of the Apostles in chapter 15."],[427.4,"What's normally called the Council of Jerusalem."],[429.73,"The first church council, the gathering of the apostles at Jerusalem, to deal with what are the elements of the Torah that are specifically applied to uh the Gentiles, the people from the nations who come into the church who aren't Jewish in background."],[447.71,"Should they, should they, do they have to keep kosher, with food?"],[451.61,"Do they have to be circumcised?"],[453.85,"That was actually the biggest one, but all of those other things too."],[457.69,"And the way they resolved that was not to set aside as a lot of people commonly today may think, was to set aside stuff and say, oh no, it's not important."],[468.13,"They actually went to the book of Leviticus."],[471.47,"The Torah. They read it very closely, took it very literally."],[476.43,"In chapters 17 through 24 of Leviticus, what's called the Holiness Code."],[480.93,"Most of the commandments are prefaced with God tells Moses, go tell the children of Israel this."],[487.57,"Go tell them to do this."],[489.33,"The children of Israel shall not do this."],[493.57,"And that's because part of what the Holiness Code is doing in Leviticus is it's setting up Israel to have a particular role with the regard. to the rest of the world"],[505.29,"Israel is chosen, but they're not chosen because God loves them and hates everybody else."],[512.01,"They're chosen to have this particular role."],[515.29,"And the role they're to have is that they're supposed to be a kingdom of priests."],[519.37,"The nation of Israel is supposed to serve as priests for the rest of the world."],[523.69,"God says, I'm going to bless the world through, he says to Abraham, I'm going to bless the world through you."],[529.42,"And then through Israel, he's going to bless the world through Israel."],[533.02,"That's also ultimately fulfilled, of course, in Christ being born a Judean."],[538.12,"So, in order to do that, they have to, as a kingdom of priests, they have to maintain their holiness at a higher level than the rest of the world."],[548.98,"If they're going to serve as priests, if they're going to be the ones, God's going to live among them, not among the other nations."],[554.82,"God's going to live in their midst, they're going to serve as priests, they have to be notches up holy."],[561.48,"Just as the priests in Israel have to take it up another notch."],[568.12,"They have to be even holier than the rest of the people of Israel."],[571.16,"Because they're going to actually come into God's presence in the tabernacle and then the temple."],[575.88,"And we still have this in the church."],[578.5,"There are rules that apply to clergy that don't apply to all the people."],[584.98,"Divorce is a sin in the Orthodox Church."],[587.94,"Clergy can't be divorced, but lay people. can be divorced and be received back into the communion of the church"],[595.98,"But if you're clergy and you get divorced, you're done as clergy."],[599.9,"So there's there are these steps, it's the same kind of thing."],[604.74,"So that's what the Holiness Code was aimed at."],[607.94,"It was aimed at."],[609.46,"Here's where Israel has to be holy beyond the other nations in order to serve this special role that would culminate in producing the Messiah."],[617.7,"But within the Holiness Code, within those chapters of Leviticus 17 through 24, there are four commandments where instead of just saying, tell the people of Israel this is what they need to do."],[630.02,"And all the food laws are in there, all of circumcision, all this is reiterated, all this other stuff we're talking about."],[636.38,"But the four things that there are four commandments where it says, Neither any of the children of Israel, nor any foreigners who live among you."],[646.06,"So if there are foreigners who aren't Israelites living among you, you don't have to make them follow all the food laws, you don't have to force them to be circumcised."],[655.12,"They can come and live in Israel without doing all that."],[657.36,"But there's four things they can't do while they're living there."],[661.12,"And those four things were eating blood, idolatry."],[667.37,"Meat sacrificed to idols, which is really part of idolatry, and sexual immorality, and the sexual immorality laws."],[674.09,"They said these four things you can't allow, and you can really reduce those into two to idolatry and sexual immorality."],[679.45,"And that's because in the Holy Spirit, those are sins that taint the land"],[681.57,"These are not to be tolerated."],[687.25,"They're so wicked that they're going to drag the whole community down."],[690.93,"So that cannot be tolerated."],[692.77,"And so in Acts 15, those are exactly the commandments that they apply to the Gentiles."],[697.89,"But the Gentiles are free to come into the church."],[701.25,"They don't have to keep all the laws regarding the food laws and all of these things."],[707.65,"But these four things can't be tolerated in the church."],[712.53,"Because the church has to be holy."],[715.25,"Because the church, as St."],[717.41,"Peter says, is a royal priesthood."],[720.57,"Same way Israel was supposed to be the church, when we celebrate the liturgy, we offer the Eucharist for the life of the world and for its salvation."],[730.57,"So we as a church are to serve as priests for the whole world, which means we need to be holy, which means we can't tolerate this."],[738.17,"So this is where St."],[739.61,"Paul is coming from."],[740.49,"He's saying, look, you can't tolerate this sexual immorality in your midst."],[746.25,"It's tainting your whole assembly when you let this happen."],[751.69,"So this isn't just a question of him sending a personal letter to this guy and saying, hey, you know, knock it off."],[760.09,"This is to the whole church."],[762.17,"You can't tolerate this kind of thing in your midst."],[766.09,"You have to deal with it."],[768.65,"You have to deal with it as a community."],[769.53,"Uh-huh There's something, because remember, he's just gotten done telling them, don't be arguing about this other stuff."],[776.11,"Don't be arguing about the food."],[777.39,"Don't be arguing about the right."],[780.03,"But this, this we need to talk about."],[785.47,"This is something that needs to be dealt with."],[789.07,"So another consequence of that, and related to this, is that sexual immorality, as it was talked about in Acts 15 at the council."],[798.25,"St. Paul talks about it here, is not a vague and nebulous thing that we can debate and negotiate."],[803.82,"What is it?"],[804.34,"Is it sexual immorality?"],[805.82,"Because they were referring directly to Leviticus chapter 18, which has all the laws."],[812.38,"One of them is you can't. sleep with your father's wife, which is what this guy is doing"],[818.74,"So notice here that Saint Paul expects them to know this."],[822.74,"He expects them to know that this is unacceptable in their midst."],[826.58,"He expects them to understand this."],[828.58,"1 Corinthians, as we said, is one of the first New Testament texts that's written."],[834.58,"So where did he expect them to know this from?"],[837.38,"Well, the Council of Jerusalem, all the time."],[840.0,"Yeah, and from the Old Testament."],[842.72,"Oh, that's a yeah."],[844.1,"From the Old Testament."],[845.22,"Yeah. So there's no whiff that the Old Testament doesn't apply."],[849.94,"Or that maybe those commandments don't apply."],[852.82,"Or we're starting over here."],[854.88,"St. Paul expects them to know this is not only that this is wrong."],[857.6,"You could say, well, even the Gentiles, as he says, even the Gentiles knew this was wrong"],[863.78,"It's not a question of knowing that this was wrong."],[865.86,"It was knowing that this can't be tolerated in the church."],[869.54,"That this is one one of very few sins that has to be tolerated."],[874.66,"So you tell you."],[875.94,"You can't say, oh, you know, Henry over there is doing this terrible thing, but we'll just ignore it."],[882.03,"I don't want to have a confrontation."],[884.19,"Yeah. You know, I'm not doing it."],[887.07,"Uh huh. I don't want to judge."],[889.47,"Yes, there's none of that."],[892.01,"Certain things, the big two being idolatry and sexual immorality."],[897.13,"And this is this is borne out in the history of the church."],[899.93,"In uh St."],[900.73,"Justinian's nomocanon, he labels idolatry and sexual immorality."],[901.13,"He he uses the language of them tainting the land"],[908.69,"He's referring to the empire."],[910.27,"But he's saying if we allow these things to go on, God will end up wiping out, judging our empire, and so we can't allow these two things to go on."],[920.89,"He didn't make every sin illegal, or everyone would have been in jail."],[924.25,"Yeah. But those two That would still be true."],[927.23,"Yeah, those two areas cannot be cannot be tolerated."],[932.35,"So St. Paul directly refers to it."],[934.83,"And that's why he refers to it to everybody."],[938.19,"Well, number one, probably everybody knew."],[940.91,"Yeah. Because that kind of thing would get around."],[944.0,"If St. Paul knew found out, that would mean people were talking."],[949.4,"But also because they need to they need to deal with it."],[953.26,"They need to deal with it."],[954.14,"That's going to be a connecting thread through several of the things he's going to talk about."],[958.22,"It's going to be the fact that the community needs to be enforcing discipline on itself."],[965.68,"That the the members of the community are responsible for each other, not just for their own behavior."],[972.16,"And so the community as a whole needs to be building people up, it needs to be correcting people."],[978.88,"They need to be corrected, needs to be bringing people to repentance and then reconciling them when they repent."],[984.56,"The community as a whole needs to be doing these things"],[990.42,"And this is a change, we saw that already in Romans."],[993.7,"That was a lot of the focus in Romans too on the whole community needing to be one and unity."],[998.9,"We tend as modern people to read everything, but including the Bible in a very individualistic way."],[1005.7,"How does this apply to me?"],[1007.22,"What is right and wrong for me to do?"],[1011.3,"That's what they teach us in Py Alphada."],[1013.3,"Yeah. And that whole idea of individualism didn't exist in the ancient world."],[1018.99,"Now, there are letters that St."],[1020.51,"Paul wrote to a person."],[1022.19,"This is not one of them."],[1023.55,"Yeah. And most of them are not written to a person."],[1026.48,"And even the ones that he writes to people are not just about that person's personal spiritual life."],[1032.16,"And walk with God"],[1035.7,"They're about things that affect other members of the community."],[1040.02,"In the case of Saints Timothy and Titus, he's sending them to churches and talking to them about what to do when they're in those churches and how to and how to operate."],[1050.42,"So again, it's this community."],[1052.02,"So devotees back to church at one removed."],[1055.14,"And so our Christian life, unless we're a hermit, which is no one in this room, and probably no one listening to this on the internet, because I don't know where a hermit would get the internet."],[1066.26,"If you're a monk who's a hermit, you live off, or a nun, you live off by yourself in the wilderness, you're not hearing this, so this isn't directed to you."],[1073.86,"The rest of us, even monastics, live our lives in a as part of a community."],[1076.42,"a community. Lead our Christian lives as part of"],[1080.54,"And you can't sit, we can't separate out our quote-unquote individual relationship with God from our life in that community and the relationship that our brothers and sisters in Christ have with Christ."],[1097.74,"And so it's important that we look at it from that perspective because that's where St."],[1102.94,"Paul's coming from in everything we're about to read."],[1105.82,"Probably for the next few sessions."],[1108.7,"He's going to be proceeding for that."],[1110.62,"It's going to be talking about that community life."],[1113.26,"So this episode of uh incest cannot be tolerated."],[1117.9,"So verse 2, and you are puffed up, and have not rather mourned that he who has done this deed might be taken away from among you."],[1127.54,"So he's talking about now how they've responded to it."],[1131.86,"So again, he's still not talking to the person."],[1134.5,"He's talking to them about how they've reacted to the person."],[1138.74,"So he says, when you've looked at this person, you've become puffed up, you've become proud, you've become arrogant."],[1144.18,"Oh, because you've looked better than that."],[1146.36,"Because you're looking, you're just looking down on him."],[1149.78,"You're just looking at this, oh what a scumbag."],[1153.08,"Sleeping with his father's wife."],[1154.76,"I mean, ugh."],[1156.12,"How shameful. And if St."],[1158.13,"Paul's heard about it, people are talking about it."],[1160.45,"He says, that's how you've approached it."],[1162.29,"He says, you should have been mourning."],[1164.69,"You should have been grieved for him."],[1165.09,"When you found out that this was happening"],[1170.71,"You know, this is terrible."],[1173.83,"You know, that he's doing this and hasn't stopped."],[1177.19,"So he's addressing the sin of their response to it, which is just as important as the sin that instigates it."],[1188.63,"The second part of the verse that he be taken away from among you is St."],[1194.71,"Paul saying you ought to you ought to cast him out."],[1198.07,"Well, we're gonna get there."],[1199.27,"Okay. Yeah. A version of that is where we're heading."],[1202.39,"Okay. But so this this is important."],[1204.95,"Remember, St. Paul is a Pharisee."],[1208.87,"That was very much how they approached sin."],[1209.67,"There were people who were sinners, and that wasn't the Pharisees"],[1216.65,"That was other people."],[1218.33,"These people are sinners."],[1219.85,"We are the righteous."],[1221.87,"Who keep the Torah and do all the right things."],[1224.35,"Yeah. These sinners over there are the problem."],[1228.83,"You can see this when Christ uh says his parable about the publican and the Pharisees."],[1233.53,"Pharisee and the publican."],[1234.65,"Yeah. Because the Pharisees are like, thank you for not making me like this awful tax collector over there."],[1240.09,"And and what Christ is so holy, I do all these good things, yeah."],[1243.29,"What Christ is always trying to explain to them."],[1245.99,"When he says it's not the the the healthy who need a doctor but the sick."],[1250.87,"He came to seek it to save the lost."],[1253.63,"What Christ is saying over and over and over again in his teaching is that the goal with the sinner is that he would turn and repent and live and be reconciled."],[1266.03,"Not to condemn him and puff yourself up with pride."],[1269.87,"And I'm emphasizing this because you will often hear the people in the Bible documentaries when they on History Channel, etc. when they talk about St."],[1278.43,"Paul, they'll say, oh, well, St."],[1280.19,"Paul never mentions any of Jesus' teaching."],[1283.39,"He just talks about his death and resurrection."],[1285.87,"Now, if you're talking about like quoting things from the Gospels, sure, the Gospels hadn't been written yet, but whatever."],[1293.01,"He doesn't quote things that are also quoted in the Gospels for the most part."],[1296.61,"There are a few times when he does, but for the most part."],[1297.33,"This is one example"],[1300.17,"But he references his teaching all over the place."],[1305.13,"If they're following Christ, they should be approaching this sinner, this grievous sinner."],[1312.49,"St. Paul isn't soft pedaling what he did."],[1316.09,"But they should be approaching it the way Christ would approach it, which is what?"],[1319.53,"Would Christ approach it by being puffed up and condemning him?"],[1322.73,"Or would Christ approach it by being grieved for him and trying to bring him back and reconcile him?"],[1330.53,"He'd be trying to work with by whatever he had to do."],[1334.5,"So, verse 3, for I indeed, as absent in body but present in spirit, have already judged as though I were present. him who has so done this deed"],[1344.46,"So he's saying, you know, I'm not there, but I've heard about this."],[1349.84,"And I've already decided what needs to be done in this situation."],[1354.65,"Which they haven't done."],[1355.85,"Uh-huh. But but St."],[1358.07,"Paul is going to tell them now what needs to happen."],[1361.67,"In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, when you are gathered together, along with my spirit, with the power of our Lord Jesus Christ, deliver such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus."],[1377.67,"So he's telling them, okay, you need to do this as an act."],[1382.01,"This is an act of the whole community."],[1385.25,"So he doesn't just say, in your heart do this or think this certain way."],[1390.47,"He's saying, when you're gathered together, as a community for worship, you need to do this in the name of the Lord Jesus as an act of the whole community."],[1400.23,"You need to deliver him over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh so that his spirit may be saved."],[1405.67,"The day of our Lord Jesus Christ, is the day when Christ will judge the living and the dead."],[1411.13,"So he's saying that's our goal."],[1413.45,"Our goal is that his soul, his life, be saved on that day, on the day of judgment."],[1420.06,"This is what we're thinking about."],[1421.26,"Not whether we get along with him right now."],[1424.44,"Not with social pleasantries right now or awkwardness right now."],[1427.92,"We're concerned. with what's going to happen to his soul on the day when he stands before the Lord"],[1433.48,"And so, in order to do that, you're going to have to right now remove him from the community."],[1441.0,"Because what he's doing can't happen in the community for the reasons we already talked about."],[1447.56,"So, what does it mean hand him over to Satan?"],[1450.04,"Yeah, that's that sounds really, really harsh."],[1453.0,"It does. That sounds really, really harsh."],[1456.38,"Uh St. Paul is here drawing on a whole understanding of what the role of demons is, in terms of why God allows them, why he has allowed them historically to exist."],[1472.62,"This imagery goes all the way back to the Torah"],[1473.75,"And he's drawing on that."],[1479.27,"So in the Torah, you've got as Israel's journey through the wilderness on their way to the land of Canaan, they're in this camp."],[1488.73,"And outside the camp, there's wilderness which is full of evil and death."],[1494.1,"And wild animals, sort of everything bad."],[1496.66,"And then within that is the camp, which they have to keep by following the Torah holy and pure, like we talked about."],[1504.28,"So that God can dwell there, can dwell there with them."],[1508.6,"So the ultimate punishment in the Torah is for someone to be cut off from among the people."],[1515.64,"That's the language it uses"],[1517.92,"And it's often unclear whether that means they're being executed or whether that means they're being sent into exile, like being thrown out of the camp."],[1526.64,"And that's because those were basically the same thing."],[1531.36,"These are seen to be the same thing."],[1533.04,"Because the place where life is, is there in the camp where God is."],[1536.08,"Yeah, they're cast out to live by themselves in the wilderness, they're not going to make it at all."],[1541.28,"But out there in the wilderness, it's not just sort of the physical harshness of it."],[1546.9,"This is the place where the demons live."],[1550.02,"And probably the most prominent example of that in Leviticus 16 is in the Day of Atonement ritual, Azazel."],[1556.34,"Azazel's out there, this demonic power out there in the wilderness."],[1560.66,"When on the Day of Atonement, they take the two goats"],[1564.3,"One of the goats is for Yahweh, the God of Israel, the other goat is for Azazel."],[1569.66,"The goat that's for Azazel, remember, they put all the sins of the community on that goat, and they send him out into the wilderness."],[1578.22,"So he takes the people's sins back out to Azazel."],[1581.84,"Back out to the demons where they belong, out of the camp."],[1585.44,"And then the other goat is then offered as a sacrifice."],[1589.76,"You can't sacrifice the goat with the sins on it, because now it's unclean and impure from the sins."],[1594.88,"But the other goat gets sacrificed to God, and its blood is used to cleanse the tabernacle, sort of the taint and residue of sin."],[1604.56,"But so this outside area, that's where these demonic powers are."],[1609.56,"Now, the origin of these demonic powers, as they believe during the Second Temple period, there are a number of versions of how they came to be."],[1619.56,"But uh one of them is in the book of Jubilees, and there's something fairly similar in 1 Enoch."],[1624.92,"Those two books, by the way, aren't the Old Testament, but in the Dead Sea Scrolls from the Qumran community, uh, those were the second and third most common texts that were found."],[1637.96,"Second and third most copies."],[1639.4,"Number one was Genesis."],[1642.44,"But two and three, above the other books of the Torah, were First Enoch and Jubilees."],[1649.24,"And in Jubilees. the demonic spirits are the spirits of dead, dead Nephilim, dead giants"],[1657.2,"But that's not the important element for these purposes."],[1659.76,"The important element for these purposes is that in explaining why they're still in the world, that God took most of those demons and threw them into the abyss."],[1670.42,"Those unclean evil spirits."],[1672.5,"But some of them, led by a fellow named Mastema, went to God and said, you know, allow us to stay in the world for a while, during this age, so that we can torment evildoers."],[1687.63,"So we won't torment any of your good people, but let us torment the evil people"],[1695.19,"And God agrees to the deal because God plans to use them to bring people to repentance."],[1703.91,"By by sort of handing them over to these evil spirits that might cause them to realize their sin and turn from it."],[1713.51,"And we see this reflected a bunch of times in the scriptures."],[1717.83,"One example is Saul, the king of Israel, at a certain point after he's disobeyed God and God has said, okay, you're not going to be king anymore, and Saul has refused to step down as king, sort of uh spite of God, the Holy Spirit leaves Saul, and we're told this unclean spirit enters into him to trouble him."],[1737.43,"And that's because, again, God is trying to take him as far as he needs in order that he might repent."],[1746.59,"And so here, St."],[1748.27,"Paul, the other Saul, is drawing on these same traditions when he talks about turning this person over to Satan for the destruction of his body so that his soul may be saved."],[1759.23,"They're saying you need to cast him out of the community."],[1762.91,"When you do that, you're cutting him off from the Eucharist, you're cutting him off from Christ, essentially."],[1770.27,"You're putting him back out into the world that's the domain of these these evil spirits, like the ones you had been worshiping that he talked about earlier."],[1778.11,"But why is that?"],[1779.15,"That's not because now we're done with him and we hate him, and he can go to the devil for all we care."],[1784.03,"can. It's because this is our last ditch effort to get him to realize he't keep living the way he's living"],[1791.75,"So if they did this, and he said, Oh, now I realize that I've done something really awful and I'm going to stop."],[1800.07,"Yes. Uh, they might not cast him out at that point."],[1803.67,"Well, no, he would be cast out, but there'd be a path back."],[1806.71,"Uh-huh. Okay. If he stopped, went through a period of repentance, he could come back into the community."],[1813.77,"Okay. Yes, that's the goal."],[1815.93,"And this is also underlies the church's whole concept of excommunication."],[1821.99,"When someone's cut off, when someone's excommunicated from the church."],[1826.39,"It's not because we hate them now and we don't want them around."],[1828.39,"It's because they're living in a way that's incompatible with them still living in the community"],[1834.99,"And that we're hoping by the drastic action that that might, you know, in our modern language cause them to hit bottom."],[1843.55,"That might cause and wake up, you know, the way the prodigal son one day sort of came to his senses."],[1849.47,"Uh-huh. You know, and came back."],[1852.27,"This might be what ultimately brings them back to."],[1854.59,"Why aren't we talking at Annie about the woman?"],[1858.91,"About the woman who is certainly sinning by having an affair with her steps."],[1865.07,"She may well have not been a member of the community."],[1867.15,"Well, that's true."],[1868.48,"Because he doesn't say that they're sleeping together."],[1871.92,"Uh-huh. He says that he's sleeping with her."],[1876.5,"Uh. So, yeah, that that would be he's the one who's doing this while a member of the community."],[1882.82,"Uh-huh. And so that makes it right, that makes it a different issue."],[1889.06,"Because again, the community is holy and needs to remain holy."],[1895.7,"So if we're going to come into God's presence, we can't do it while we're in the midst of sin."],[1902.26,"He's going to talk about the Eucharist in the same kind of way, very specifically in a few chapters."],[1907.58,"Um but here, this is such a gross sin."],[1911.46,"It's beyond even just you could stay in the community, but we need to not receive the Eucharist for a while."],[1917.18,"He.'s gonna talk about that later"],[1919.02,"This is you know, you can't even be a part of while you're doing something like this, sort of openly and notoriously."],[1927.5,"I I know of a couple of instances in which people have done something or are doing something that they themselves think is so wrong that they leave the church."],[1942.51,"And it seems like in this case that maybe he would do that, but obviously he hasn't."],[1948.43,"He hasn't. Uh but uh when i is that the right thing for them to do in their of course the right thing is for them to stop doing whatever it is."],[1960.26,"Yeah, I have to"],[1962.62,"We'll talk about this more when we get to the Eucharist related to that, because there's also people who do that who just decide I'm not going to take the Eucharist."],[1972.54,"And we'll talk about that element more there."],[1974.7,"But this, I think it's important that St."],[1976.54,"Paul says this has to be a community action."],[1979.67,"He's concerned with the community's action rather than the individual."],[1983.27,"This has to be a uh community action."],[1987.19,"I mean, is that the right thing to do?"],[1988.71,"The right thing to do is to stop committing the sin."],[1991.51,"Yeah. Um that's I mean, that's the right thing to do."],[1995.8,"So I it's hard to imagine how St."],[1997.72,"Paul would address."],[1998.8,"Well, if I'm not going to stop committing incest, should I leave the church voluntarily?"],[2005.44,"Yeah, I don't know how we would even address such a question."],[2009.12,"It would be, you need to stop committing incest."],[2013.34,"But it sounds like here he would still want the community to go through."],[2019.26,"Yes. Yeah, even if he didn't show up."],[2021.92,"Yes. Even if he's gone, they should do it."],[2024.72,"They need to do this to purify."],[2027.76,"Officially. And this is and this is also a way of them praying for him."],[2033.76,"So the community is taking an action."],[2036.56,"The community's not just like, ugh, and then he leaves, and they're like, oh, he left."],[2042.56,"Now we don't have to deal with ha.ha"],[2044.8,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scri.ptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[2052.12,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2057.4,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":24.4,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: factions, humility, and the move to specifics","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Corinthians 5</b> - \"from the heading, it's gonna get <b>interesting</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So far: <b>introduction</b> to the letter, Paul on his <b>own role</b></li><li class=\"d1\">arguing against <b>factions</b> - followers of individual apostles → <b>dissension, division</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his ministry and its <b>humility</b>: <b>nothing to boast about</b> → don't become his fan or follower</li><li class=\"d1\">applies to the apostles in general: <b>emulate the humility</b> with which he served Christ → \"very much on the right track\"</li></ul>"},{"t":104.52,"lv":2,"h":"Transition point: Timothy and the coming visit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Now a <b>transition</b>: Paul about to get into <b>particular issues</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of ch. 4: sending <b>Timothy</b> as <b>representative</b> - remind them of what Paul taught and <b>how he lived among them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">preparatory to his <b>visit</b> - \"a threat and a promise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'd like my visit <b>warm and wonderful</b>, but if some things don't change, <b>unpleasant</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":147.76,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Is this still the theology half?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: usually letters begin with <b>general theology</b> then <b>particular applications</b> - this seems particular all along</li><li class=\"d1\">answer: <b>still the theology portion</b>; will get <b>even more specific</b> later</li><li class=\"d0\">This letter is different: <b>serious problems at Corinth</b> → even the theology is <b>more focused</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans</b>: one main theme - reconciling <b>Jewish and Gentile believers</b> in one body</li><li class=\"d1\">theology and application both aimed at that <b>one overarching theme</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Corinth</b>: still two big halves, but each covers <b>a whole bunch of different issues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lay groundwork on many issues, then apply to many - \"instead of one central thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some epistles written to <b>one person</b> - still two halves, but <b>different</b> - talking to one person vs a community</li></ul>"},{"t":275.89,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 5:1 - Sexual immorality among you","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:1</b>: \"sexual immorality among you... not even named among the <b>Gentiles</b>, that a man has his <b>father's wife</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is bad\" - Paul <b>cuts to the chase</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gentiles = <b>nations</b> - point: not one of the <b>pagan-convert adaptation</b> issues</li><li class=\"d1\">much of Corinth's trouble: converts from <b>paganism</b> → huge gap between <b>Roman pagan religion</b> and Christianity (an extension of the <b>Jewish world</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">things once <b>appropriate</b> now <b>wildly inappropriate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this is <b>not one of those</b>: nobody thinks it's okay to sleep with your <b>stepmother</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>shameful even for complete pagans</b></li></ul>"},{"t":375.89,"lv":3,"h":"The problem is the community's inaction","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"taking place <b>among you</b>\" - <b>in your midst</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one particular person doing the act</li><li class=\"d1\">problem isn't just one person sinning - it's <b>sinning in the community, and the community not doing anything</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not one example among many (others come) - <b>this particular case</b></li></ul>"},{"t":416.55,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 15 and the Holiness Code","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>Acts 15</b>: <b>Council of Jerusalem</b> - first church council</li><li class=\"d1\">which parts of the <b>Torah</b> apply to <b>Gentiles</b> who come into the church?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>kosher</b> food? <b>circumcision</b> (biggest one)?</li><li class=\"d0\">Not resolved by setting things aside as \"not important\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they went to <b>Leviticus 17-24</b> = the <b>Holiness Code</b>, read closely and <b>literally</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most commandments prefaced: \"go tell the <b>children of Israel</b>...\"</li></ul>"},{"t":493.57,"lv":3,"h":"Israel as a kingdom of priests","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Holiness Code sets Israel up for a <b>particular role</b> toward the rest of the world</li><li class=\"d1\">chosen - <b>not</b> because God loves them and hates everybody else</li><li class=\"d1\">role: <b>kingdom of priests</b> for the rest of the world</li><li class=\"d1\">God to Abraham: \"<b>bless the world through you</b>\" → through Israel → fulfilled in <b>Christ born a Judean</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Priests must keep <b>holiness at a higher level</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God lives <b>in their midst</b>; \"notches up holy\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Israel's priests go <b>another notch</b> - they enter God's presence in <b>tabernacle, temple</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Still in the church: <b>rules for clergy</b> not for all</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>divorce</b> is a sin; laypeople divorced can be <b>received back into communion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">clergy who divorce: \"you're done as clergy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Holiness Code aimed at Israel being holy <b>beyond the nations</b> → culminating in the <b>Messiah</b></li></ul>"},{"t":617.7,"lv":3,"h":"Four commandments for foreigners","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Four commandments say: \"<b>neither any of the children of Israel, nor any foreigners</b> who live among you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">foreigners need <b>not</b> keep food laws or be <b>circumcised</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>four things</b> they can't do:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>eating blood</b>, <b>idolatry</b>, <b>meat sacrificed to idols</b> (part of idolatry), <b>sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reduces to <b>two: idolatry and sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why: in the Holy Spirit, these <b>taint the land</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so wicked they <b>drag the whole community down</b> - cannot be tolerated</li></ul>"},{"t":692.77,"lv":3,"h":"The church is a royal priesthood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b> applies exactly these to Gentiles: free of food laws etc., but <b>these four can't be tolerated in the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the church must be <b>holy</b>: <b>royal priesthood</b> (<b>St. Peter</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">same way <b>Israel</b> was supposed to be</li><li class=\"d0\">Liturgy: offer the <b>Eucharist</b> for the life of the world → church = <b>priests for the whole world</b> → must be holy → <b>can't tolerate this</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: sexual immorality <b>taints your whole assembly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a <b>personal letter</b> to the guy: \"knock it off\"</li><li class=\"d1\">addressed to the <b>whole church</b> - <b>deal with it as a community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">just told them <b>not to argue</b> about food etc. - \"but <b>this</b> we need to talk about\"</li></ul>"},{"t":789.07,"lv":2,"h":"Sexual immorality is defined, not debatable","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sexual immorality in Acts 15 / here = <b>not vague</b>, not negotiable</li><li class=\"d1\">refers to <b>Leviticus 18</b>: can't sleep with your <b>father's wife</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>expects them to know</b> this</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Corinthians = one of the <b>first NT texts</b> written → where from? <b>Council of Jerusalem</b> and the <b>Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"no whiff\" that the OT or those commandments <b>don't apply</b>, or \"we're starting over\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not that they'd know it's wrong (\"even the Gentiles\") - but that it <b>can't be tolerated in the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"one of very few sins\" that can't be</li><li class=\"d1\">can't say \"Henry's doing this terrible thing, but we'll <b>ignore it</b>... I don't want a confrontation... I don't want to <b>judge</b>\" - <b>none of that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The big two: <b>idolatry and sexual immorality</b></li></ul>"},{"t":897.13,"lv":3,"h":"Justinian's nomocanon; community discipline","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Justinian's nomocanon</b>: idolatry and sexual immorality <b>taint the land</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he means the <b>empire</b> - allow them, God will <b>judge</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't make <b>every sin illegal</b> (everyone in jail) - but <b>those two areas</b> can't be tolerated</li><li class=\"d0\">Why tell everybody?</li><li class=\"d1\">probably <b>everybody knew</b> - \"that kind of thing gets around\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but they <b>need to deal with it</b> - <b>connecting thread</b> of several coming topics</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Community enforces discipline on itself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">members <b>responsible for each other</b>, not just own behavior</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>build up, correct, bring to repentance, reconcile</b> when they repent</li></ul>"},{"t":990.42,"lv":2,"h":"Community vs modern individualism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Already in <b>Romans</b>: whole community in <b>unity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moderns read everything, <b>even the Bible, individualistically</b>: \"how does this apply to <b>me</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>individualism didn't exist</b> in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d0\">Letters to a person (<b>Timothy, Titus</b>) - <b>not</b> about their <b>personal spiritual life and walk with God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">about things affecting others; sending them to <b>churches</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christian life = <b>life in community</b>, unless a <b>hermit</b> - \"no one in this room... where would a hermit get the internet?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">even <b>monastics</b> live in community</li><li class=\"d1\">can't separate our \"individual relationship with God\" from <b>community life</b> and <b>brothers and sisters' relationship with Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Where Paul is coming from for <b>the next few sessions</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1113.26,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 5:2 - Puffed up instead of mourning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:2</b>: \"you are <b>puffed up</b>, and have not rather <b>mourned</b> that he who has done this deed might be <b>taken away</b> from among you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">still not speaking to the sinner - to them about <b>how they reacted</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they became <b>proud, arrogant</b> - \"what a <b>scumbag</b>... how shameful\"</li><li class=\"d1\">should have been <b>mourning</b>, <b>grieved for him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Addressing <b>the sin of their response</b> - \"just as important as the sin that instigates it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"taken away from among you\" = cast him out - \"<b>a version of that</b> is where we're heading\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1204.95,"lv":2,"h":"The Pharisee mindset versus Christ's teaching","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul is a <b>Pharisee</b>: sinners are <b>other people</b>; \"we are the righteous\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pharisee and publican</b> parable: \"thank you for not making me like that tax collector\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ over and over: \"not the healthy who need a doctor but the sick\" - came to <b>save the lost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goal with the sinner: <b>turn, repent, live, be reconciled</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> to condemn him and <b>puff yourself up</b></li><li class=\"d0\">History Channel documentaries: \"<b>Paul never mentions Jesus' teaching</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Gospels not written yet; he doesn't quote them much, but <b>references his teaching all over</b> - this is one example</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>isn't soft-pedaling</b> what he did</li><li class=\"d1\">but approach it as <b>Christ</b> would: <b>puffed up and condemning</b>, or <b>grieved, bringing him back, reconciling</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":1334.5,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 5:3-5 - Deliver such a one to Satan","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:3</b>: \"absent in body but present in spirit, have <b>already judged</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">decided what needs doing - which they haven't</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:4-5</b>: \"in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, when you are <b>gathered together</b>... deliver such a one to <b>Satan</b> for the <b>destruction of the flesh</b>, that his <b>spirit may be saved</b> in the day of the Lord Jesus\"</li><li class=\"d1\">an <b>act of the whole community</b>, \"when gathered for <b>worship</b>\" - not just a thought in the heart</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Day of the Lord Jesus</b> = Christ judges <b>living and dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goal: his <b>soul saved</b> on that day</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>getting along</b> now, <b>social pleasantries, awkwardness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">must <b>remove him from the community</b> now</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"hand him over to Satan\" - <b>sounds really harsh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul draws on an understanding of <b>why God allows demons</b> to exist - back to the <b>Torah</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1447.56,"lv":2,"h":"Camp and wilderness: cut off, Azazel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israel in the <b>wilderness camp</b>: outside = <b>evil, death, wild animals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">inside: camp kept <b>holy and pure</b> so God can dwell with them</li><li class=\"d0\">Ultimate punishment: <b>cut off from among the people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unclear: <b>executed or exiled</b>? - <b>same thing</b> - life is where God is; cast out, \"not going to make it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Wilderness = <b>place where demons live</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Leviticus 16, Day of Atonement</b>: <b>Azazel</b>, demonic power in the wilderness</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two goats</b>: one for <b>Yahweh</b>, one for <b>Azazel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sins of the community on Azazel's goat → sent out \"<b>back to the demons where they belong</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">other goat <b>sacrificed</b>; blood <b>cleanses the tabernacle</b> (taint and residue of sin) - can't sacrifice a goat with sins on it</li></ul>"},{"t":1604.56,"lv":3,"h":"Origin of demons: Jubilees, 1 Enoch, Mastema","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second Temple versions of demon origins: <b>Jubilees</b>, similar in <b>1 Enoch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not OT, but at <b>Qumran</b> (Dead Sea Scrolls): <b>second and third most copies</b> after <b>Genesis</b>, above other Torah books</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jubilees</b>: demons = spirits of dead <b>Nephilim, giants</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why still in the world: God threw most into the <b>abyss</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some, led by <b>Mastema</b>, asked to stay \"for a while, during this age\" to <b>torment evildoers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we won't torment any of your good people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God agrees - plans to use them to bring people to <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">handing people over to evil spirits → realize their sin, <b>turn from it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1713.51,"lv":2,"h":"Saul and Saul: excommunication as last-ditch mercy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reflected in scripture: <b>King Saul</b> - disobeyed, refused to step down → <b>Holy Spirit leaves</b>, an <b>unclean spirit</b> enters to trouble him</li><li class=\"d1\">God taking him as far as needed so he might <b>repent</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul, \"the other Saul\"</b>, draws on same traditions: turn over to Satan for <b>destruction of the body</b>, soul saved</li><li class=\"d1\">cast out = cut off from the <b>Eucharist</b>, from <b>Christ</b>, back to the <b>domain of evil spirits</b> (like those they'd worshiped)</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"we hate him, he can <b>go to the devil</b>\" - it's the <b>last-ditch effort</b> to wake him up</li><li class=\"d0\">If he repents: <b>a path back</b> - period of repentance, then <b>return to the community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Underlies <b>excommunication</b>: not hatred, but living <b>incompatibly</b> with the community</li><li class=\"d1\">hope it causes him to \"<b>hit bottom</b>\" - like the <b>prodigal son</b> coming to his senses</li></ul>"},{"t":1854.59,"lv":2,"h":"Q: What about the woman? The Eucharist","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: why not address <b>the woman</b> - she's also sinning</li><li class=\"d1\">she may <b>not have been a member</b> of the community</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul says <b>he's</b> sleeping with her - he's the member doing this</li><li class=\"d1\">community is <b>holy</b> and must remain so → can't come into God's presence <b>in the midst of sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eucharist</b> discussed the same way <b>in a few chapters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this sin so gross it's beyond \"stay in community but <b>don't receive</b> for a while\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't be a part while doing it <b>openly and notoriously</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1927.5,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Leaving the church voluntarily","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: people who think what they're doing is so wrong they <b>leave the church</b> - right thing?</li><li class=\"d1\">of course the right thing is to <b>stop doing it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also people who decide \"<b>I'm not going to take the Eucharist</b>\" - more when we reach the Eucharist chapters</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: must be a <b>community action</b> - concerned with the <b>community, not the individual</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hard to imagine Paul answering \"if I won't stop <b>committing incest</b>, should I leave?\" - answer: <b>stop</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even if he's <b>gone</b>, the community should still do it - <b>to purify</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>a way of praying for him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"ugh... oh he left, <b>now we don't have to deal with it</b>\"</li></ul>"}]});
